Part A — Integration challenge
Build a small analysis program from the following requirements:

A shop has a list of transaction values. Transactions above a threshold receive a configurable discount. The program should calculate the average final transaction value.

Before code:

define inputs;
define the transformation;
decide whether a function is useful;
identify at least three test cases.

Independent integration / Lab Evidence
Create a small transaction analysis program that:

uses at least one function;
processes a collection;
contains at least one decision;
produces a summary value;
includes three tests;
explains one potential silent logic error;
includes the Week 14 AI Verify diagnosis.

Homework / Portfolio close
Submit one revised program from Weeks 8–14 with:

the original problem;
the algorithm;
the final code;
one bug or weakness you found;
the test that exposed it;
the corrected version;
a 100–150 word verification reflection.

This is the final TRACE evidence package for the course.

Programs often receive data from outside the code itself.

How do we move from temporary variables to data that already exists in a file?




open text files safely;
read line-based data;
write output;
use with open(...);
clean newline characters;
test file-derived values before trusting downstream comparisons.

